{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790513060,"nonce":"jh2IpZtn3sXbZicPVLckw+Vb34Q=","message":"Design notes from the caribou exchange (caribou d230d66 does this on its heap): a wasm collection is safe only when every frame below it is compiled Haxe pinned to the shadow stack. So defer in-allocation collections on wasm and run them at loop polls (bump the poll epoch on EVERY deferral; one bump before a function starts is missed). Holes to cover: (1) ash_std functions that call back into compiled code (sort comparators, closure runners, host after/init) put a runtime frame holding wasm locals below the poll; count runtime depth and defer while it is nonzero; (2) code that never polls (loop-free recursion, long ash_std phases) piles deferrals up to the hard backstop; allocation entry points called directly from compiled code at runtime depth 0 are as safe as a poll and restore real per-allocation stress; (3) the hard backstop on wasm should try memory.grow before collecting in place.","files":null}]}